--------------------|\n| 1 | \"~16 component action files\" (mislabeled) | Exactly 15 action files across four tiers: 8 component + 3 service + 1 DSC + 3 module |\n| 2 | Architecture description omitted the module controller tier | Module controller with 8 module handlers (Dashboard, AIGateway, KServe, OGX, Workbenches, MLflowOperator, FeastOperator, MCPLifecycleOperator) is in scope; covered by a dedicated P0 HLR |\n| 3 | Open question premised on uncertain action-file count | Resolved by SME (8/8 components have exactly one action file) — no Investigation epic generated for it |\n\nSME confirmations followed without change: the three-problem diagnosis (message-embedded\nidentity, DSCI non-standard keys, missing resourceKind), DSCI key names\n(`Request.Name`/`DSCInitialization`), the 5 controller tiers, the 5 service controllers\nincluding Setup, cloudmanager's shared reconciler already providing name/namespace, camelCase\nfield naming, the cluster-scoped resource AC, and the S effort estimate.\n\n### Component mapping (canonical names)\n\n`rhods-operator` is not verbatim in `.context/rhai-components.txt`. Mappings used:\n\n| Strategy component | Canonical component assigned | Rationale |\n|--------------------|------------------------------|-----------|\n| rhods-operator (manager binary, cloudmanager binary, CI lint rule) | OpenShift AI Productization | rhods-operator is the downstream RHOAI platform operator (red-hat-data-services/rhods-operator, konflux builds, bundle-sync workflows per architecture context) — the closest canonical name for platform-operator productization work |\n| Contribution guideline documentation | Documentation | docs-authoring work product (new guideline doc topic) |\n\n### Open question dispositions (Step 3 scan)\n\nAll four open questions were evaluated against the decision rule \"does the answer change which\ndownstream epics exist or what they do?\" — none qualifies for an Investigation epic:\n\n| Open question | Disposition |\n|----