e); it does not need the conversion landed to be written or validated against fixtures |\n\n## HLR Traceability Matrix\n\n| HLR | Priority | Requirement | Epic | Notes |\n|-----|----------|-------------|------|-------|\n| HLR-1 | P0 | All rhods-operator reconciliation error logs embedding resource name/namespace in message strings converted to structured KV pairs (`name`, `namespace`) | RHAISTRAT-8010-E001, RHAISTRAT-8010-E002, RHAISTRAT-8010-E003 (enforcement) | E001 covers the manager binary, E002 the cloudmanager binary (both binaries ship in rhods-operator); E003 adds the CI lint rule that prevents regression and serves as the AC measurement mechanism |\n| HLR-2 | P0 | DSCI controller migrated from `Request.Name`/`DSCInitialization` to camelCase `name`/`namespace` | RHAISTRAT-8010-E001 | DSCI is a manager-binary controller |\n| HLR-3 | P0 | Service controllers (Gateway, Auth, Monitoring, CertConfigMapGenerator, Setup) include `resourceKind` | RHAISTRAT-8010-E001 | manager-binary service controllers |\n| HLR-4 | P0 | All 15 action-file error logs across 4 tiers include `name`, `namespace`, `resourceKind` | RHAISTRAT-8010-E001 | all 15 action files (8 component + 3 service + 1 DSC + 3 module) live in the manager binary |\n| HLR-5 | P0 | Cloudmanager controllers (AWSKubernetesEngine, AzureKubernetesEngine, CoreWeaveKubernetesEngine) include `name`, `namespace`, `resourceKind` | RHAISTRAT-8010-E002 | cloudmanager binary; name/namespace already provided by the shared reconciler — remaining work is embedded-identity conversion plus `resourceKind` |\n| HLR-6 | P0 | Module controller error logs (8 module handlers) include `name`, `namespace`, `resourceKind` | RHAISTRAT-8010-E001 | module controller tier added by SME input |\n| HLR-7 | P1 | DSC and component controller reconciliation error logs include `resourceKind` | RHAISTRAT-8010-E001 (bundled) | Bundled with the P0 work: HLR-7 edits the same log.Error call sites in the same DSC/component controller files as the HLR-1 conversion pass; `resourceKind` is added in the same mechanical edit, so a split would double-touch the same files and duplicate the call-site audit. |\n| HLR-8 | P1 | Document the convention as a contribution guideline and publish for external operator adoption | RHAISTRAT-8010-E004 | docs-authoring epic; priority from strategy frontmatter (Major → P1) per the docs-authoring priority exception |\n\nAll eight HLRs are covered; no orphaned requirements.\n\n## Health Warnings\n\n- **Planning note (epic size)**: the manager binary log standardization epic\n  (RHAISTRAT-8010-E001) is ~1.25–1.5 sprints by the strategy's own effort estimate\n  (0.5 sprint audit + 0.75–1 sprint conversion) — above the ~2-week epic-size guideline.\n  Kept as one logical change: a split would duplicate the call-site audit and fragment a single\n  mechanical conversion over one surface. **Re-split trigger**: re-split this epic if the audit\n  finds materially more `log.Error` call sites than the strategy's 75%-of-calls estimate\n  implies, or if PR-review throughput forces it — the natural split lines are the five\n  controller tiers / the 15 action files.\n- **External adoption is not controllable**: COO team adoption (Loki Operator, Tempo Operator)\n  is voluntary per platform policy overlay 0008. The decomposition publishes the convention and\n  files tracking issues; adoption timing and occurrence are external and must not be used as a\n  gate or acceptance criterion on any epic.\n- **Process gates pending**: all Prerequisites & Process Gates in the strategy (Architecture\n  Review, Build Onboarding, Licence Validation, etc.) are \"Pending human review\". The component\n  is established in the platform (architecture context exists; Konflux builds exist), so these\n  read as strategy-signoff formalities rather than generators of onboarding epics — but they\n  remain unresolved human gates on the strategy itself.\n- **Log consumer regression risk**: message strings change (resource identity moves out of\n  message text) and the DSCI key migration renames fields that existing Loki/CloudWatch queries\n  may filter on. Mitigation is in scope as acceptance criteria on both implementation epics\n  (before/after ingestion verification, SRE/Support alert-rule identification); the DSCI\n  migration must land in a single release to avoid long-term inconsistency.\n- **Open product decision**: whether the convention should grow fields beyond\n  name/namespace/kind (reconciliation attempt count, error category) is unresolved (PM /\n  Observability). The guideline documents the three required fields; an extension is follow-up\n  scope and does not gate this decomposition.\n\n## Tiered Delivery\n\nNot applicable in the dependency-gated sense: no external dependency blocks RHOAI-side work\n(the only external dependency, COO adoption, is out of RHOAI's control entirely and is not a\ndeliverable of any epic). Sequencing flexibility exists by construction: the cloudmanager epic\nis independent of the manager epic and has no downstream dependents besides the guideline, so\nit can be scheduled into the same release or a subsequent one (the strategy's open release-timing\nquestion) without restructuring the DAG.\n"}}],"model":"rits/zai-org/glm-5-3","stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":66527,"output_tokens":0},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-aa16b0116ecc4ba6","agent_id":"ab5882413e4b7889e","session_id":"fa7e0b7e-f01f-43d2-b53c-0d6134573784","uuid":"729f87c2-f2c6-46e0-8c51-f37207ae84c9","timestamp":"2026-10-25T20:46:16.590Z","subagent_type":"claude","task_description":"Decompose RHAISTRAT-8010"}
