ngest both formats without configuration changes.\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n\n- Static analysis over the cloudmanager binary reports zero reconciliation-path `log.Error`\n  calls embedding resource name or namespace in the message string.\n- Unit tests assert the reconciliation error logs of all three cloudmanager controllers\n  (AWSKubernetesEngine, AzureKubernetesEngine, CoreWeaveKubernetesEngine) include `name`,\n  `namespace`, and `resourceKind` with correct values.\n- Unit tests confirm cluster-scoped infrastructure resources emit `name` and `resourceKind`\n  and no spurious `namespace` value.\n- A before/after log output comparison for a sample reconciliation error is documented,\n  showing structured fields present and both old and new formats ingested by the cloud log\n  consumer without configuration changes.\n\n## HLR Traceability\n\n- **HLR-1 (P0)**: conversion of message-embedded resource identity to structured KV pairs —\n  cloudmanager binary portion.\n- **HLR-5 (P0)**: `name`/`namespace`/`resourceKind` in all cloudmanager controller\n  reconciliation error logs.\n\n## Risks / Notes\n\n- **Release timing**: whether this work ships in the same release as the manager-binary\n  conversion or a subsequent one is an open product decision. This epic is independent of the\n  manager-binary work and has no downstream dependents besides the convention guideline, so\n  either sequencing works without restructuring.\n- **Narrower impact scope**: cloudmanager is only deployed on non-OpenShift Kubernetes (AWS\n  EKS, Azure AKS, CoreWeave), so the log-consumer regression surface (CloudWatch rather than\n  Loki) is narrower than the manager binary's.\n- Per the strategy's effort estimate this epic is ~0.25 sprint — well within the epic-size\n  guideline.\n"}}],"model":"rits/zai-org/glm-5-3","stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":72870,"output_tokens":0},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-aa16b0116ecc4ba6","agent_i