{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-9b5ce4645fb6b098","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t---\n2\tparent_strat: RHAISTRAT-8010\n3\tepic_count: 4\n4\tcritical_path_length: 2\n5\tdecompose_complete: true\n6\trevised: false\n7\ttriage: null\n8\ttriage_rationale: null\n9\t---\n10\t# Decomposition Summary — RHAISTRAT-8010\n11\t\n12\tOperator reconciliation log standardization with structured resource context.\n13\t\n14\t## Decomposition Notes\n15\t\n16\t### Triage analysis (Step 0)\n17\t\n18\tThe strategy is S-sized, affects a single component (rhods-operator), and is owned by a single\n19\tteam (ODH Operator team) — but the below-threshold path does not apply because the scope spans\n20\tmultiple architecturally distinct work streams that warrant separate PR review cycles:\n21\t\n22\t1. **Code implementation** — a mechanical conversion of reconciliation error log calls across two\n23\t   binaries (manager, cloudmanager) of the same repository.\n24\t2. **CI tooling** — a static-analysis/lint rule enforcing the convention going forward.\n25\t3. **Substantive documentation authoring** — a new contribution guideline (a new doc topic with\n26\t   rationale, before/after examples, and a migration guide), plus publication for external\n27\t   operator adoption. This is substantive content authoring, not an incidental documentation\n28\t   touch-up falling out of a code change.\n29\t\n30\tThe manager and cloudmanager binaries are also architecturally distinct sub-systems (different\n31\tentrypoints, different deployment targets — OpenShift vs. AWS EKS / Azure AKS / CoreWeave),\n32\twhich independently supports splitting them into separate epics.\n33\t\n34\t### Staff Engineer / SME Input (takes precedence — overrides logged)\n35\t\n36\tThe Staff Engineer / SME Input is non-empty and substantive. It was parsed first and takes\n37\tprecedence over the AI-generated Technical Approach. Overrides applied:\n38\t\n39\t| # | AI-generated text | SME correction (followed) |\n40\t|---|------------------|---------------------------|\n41\t| 1 | \"~16 component action files\" (mislabeled) | Exactly 15 action files across four tiers: 8 component + 3 service + 1 DSC + 3 module |\n42\t| 2 | Architecture description omitted the module controller tier | Module controller with 8 module handlers (Dashboard, AIGateway, KServe, OGX, Workbenches, MLflowOperator, FeastOperator, MCPLifecycleOperator) is in scope; covered by a dedicated P0 HLR |\n43\t| 3 | Open question premised on uncertain action-file count | Resolved by SME (8/8 components have exactly one action file) — no Investigation epic generated for it |\n44\t\n45\tSME confirmations followed without change: the three-problem diagnosis (message-embedded\n46\tidentity, DSCI non-standard keys, missing resourceKind), DSCI key names\n47\t(`Request.Name`/`DSCInitialization`), the 5 controller tiers, the 5 service controllers\n48\tincluding Setup, cloudmanager's shared reconciler already providing name/namespace, camelCase\n49\tfield naming, the cluster-scoped resource AC, and the S effort estimate.\n50\t\n51\t### Component mapping (canonical names)\n52\t\n53\t`rhods-operator` is not verbatim in `.context/rhai-components.txt`. Mappings used:\n54\t\n55\t| Strategy component | Canonical component assigned | Rationale |\n56\t|--------------------|------------------------------|-----------|\n57\t| rhods-operator (manager binary, cloudmanager binary, CI lint rule) | OpenShift AI Productization | rhods-operator is the downstream RHOAI platform operator (red-hat-data-services/rhods-operator, konflux builds, bundle-sync workflows per architecture context) — the closest canonical name for platform-operator productization work |\n58\t| Contribution guideline documentation | Documentation | docs-authoring work product (new guideline doc topic) |\n59\t\n60\t### Open question dispositions (Step 3 scan)\n61\t\n62\tAll four open questions were evaluated against the decision rule \"does the answer change which\n63\tdownstream epics exist or what they do?\" — none qualifies for an Investigation epic:\n64\t\n65\t| Open question | Disposition |\n66\t|---------------|-------------|\n67\t| Should the convention include fields beyond name/namespace/kind? (PM / Observability) | Non-gating: the required fields are pinned by the P0/P1 HLRs. Captured as a note on the guideline epic; any field extension is follow-up scope. |\n68\t| Which existing log-based alerts rely on message-string patterns? (SRE / Support) | Non-gating: resolved during the log-consumer regression validation — acceptance criterion on both implementation epics (SRE/Support coordination is part of that work). |\n69\t| Cloudmanager same release or subsequent? (PM / ODH Operator) | Non-gating scheduling decision: the cloudmanager work is already a separate epic with no downstream dependents (other than docs), so either answer is pure sequencing. |\n70\t| Actual percentage of log calls embedding identity? (ODH Operator) | Non-gating implementation detail: the call-site audit is the first step of the conversion work itself; the conversion proceeds identically whether the true figure is higher or lower than the 75% estimate. |\n71\t\n72\t### Passive / external components\n73\t\n74\tThe Cluster Observability Operator team (Loki Operator, Tempo Operator) is a passive recipient:\n75\tper platform policy overlay 0008, RHOAI does not install, manage, or control external operator\n76\tdependencies, and the RFE approval comment explicitly confirms voluntary adoption. No COO\n77\tvalidation or implementation epic is generated; publication of the convention (and filing\n78\ttracking issues in COO projects) is scope of the guideline epic. COO adoption itself is\n79\texternally controlled and is recorded as a health warning, not an acceptance criterion.\n80\t\n81\t### New component detection (Step 2)\n82\t\n83\trhods-operator has an architecture context file\n84\t(`.context/architecture-context/architecture/rhoai-3.6-ea.1/rhods-operator.md`) — the component\n85\tis in the platform. No onboarding chain (repo-onboarding / license-validation /\n86\tkonflux-onboarding) is generated; the repo already builds via Konflux\n87\t(`Dockerfiles/Dockerfile.konflux` per architecture context).\n88\t\n89\t## Epic List\n90\t\n91\t| Epic | Title | Type | Component | Team | Priority |\n92\t|------|-------|------|-----------|------|----------|\n93\t| RHAISTRAT-8010-E001 | Manager binary structured reconciliation logging (name/namespace/resourceKind) | Implementation | OpenShift AI Productization | ODH Operator team | P0 |\n94\t| RHAISTRAT-8010-E002 | Cloudmanager binary structured reconciliation logging | Implementation | OpenShift AI Productization | ODH Operator team | P0 |\n95\t| RHAISTRAT-8010-E003 | CI lint rule enforcing structured log fields | Implementation | OpenShift AI Productization | ODH Operator team | P1 |\n96\t| RHAISTRAT-8010-E004 | Structured logging convention contribution guideline | Implementation (docs-authoring) | Documentation | ODH Operator team | P1 |\n97\t\n98\t## Dependency DAG\n99\t\n100\t```mermaid\n101\tgraph TD\n102\t    E001[\"RHAISTRAT-8010-E001<br/>Manager binary log standardization\"] --> E004[\"RHAISTRAT-8010-E004<br/>Convention guideline (docs)\"]\n103\t    E002[\"RHAISTRAT-8010-E002<br/>Cloudmanager binary log standardization\"] --> E004\n104\t    E003[\"RHAISTRAT-8010-E003<br/>CI lint rule\"] --> E004\n105\t```\n106\t\n107\t## DAG Justification\n108\t\n109\t| Edge / non-edge | Rule | Rationale |\n110\t|-----------------|------|-----------|\n111\t| RHAISTRAT-8010-E001 → RHAISTRAT-8010-E004 | Rule 11 (docs last) | docs-authoring epic blocked by every Implementation epic; the guideline documents what was built |\n112\t| RHAISTRAT-8010-E002 → RHAISTRAT-8010-E004 | Rule 11 (docs last) | same |\n113\t| RHAISTRAT-8010-E003 → RHAISTRAT-8010-E004 | Rule 11 (docs last) | same |\n114\t| No edge RHAISTRAT-8010-E001 ↔ RHAISTRAT-8010-E002 | Rule 15 (same repo, different areas) | manager and cloudmanager are separate binaries/entrypoints touching disjoint controller sets; merge-conflict risk is coordination, not dependency |\n115\t| No edge RHAISTRAT-8010-E003 ↔ RHAISTRAT-8010-E001/E002 | Rule 14 (independent deliverables) | the lint rule codifies the field convention already pinned by the HLRs (name, namespace, resourceKind, camelCase); it does not need the conversion landed to be written or validated against fixtures |\n116\t\n117\t## HLR Traceability Matrix\n118\t\n119\t| HLR | Priority | Requirement | Epic | Notes |\n120\t|-----|----------|-------------|------|-------|\n121\t| HLR-1 | P0 | All rhods-operator reconciliation error logs embedding resource name/namespace in message strings converted to structured KV pairs (`name`, `namespace`) | RHAISTRAT-8010-E001, RHAISTRAT-8010-E002, RHAISTRAT-8010-E003 (enforcement) | E001 covers the manager binary, E002 the cloudmanager binary (both binaries ship in rhods-operator); E003 adds the CI lint rule that prevents regression and serves as the AC measurement mechanism |\n122\t| HLR-2 | P0 | DSCI controller migrated from `Request.Name`/`DSCInitialization` to camelCase `name`/`namespace` | RHAISTRAT-8010-E001 | DSCI is a manager-binary controller |\n123\t| HLR-3 | P0 | Service controllers (Gateway, Auth, Monitoring, CertConfigMapGenerator, Setup) include `resourceKind` | RHAISTRAT-8010-E001 | manager-binary service controllers |\n124\t| HLR-4 | P0 | All 15 action-file error logs across 4 tiers include `name`, `namespace`, `resourceKind` | RHAISTRAT-8010-E001 | all 15 action files (8 component + 3 service + 1 DSC + 3 module) live in the manager binary |\n125\t| HLR-5 | P0 | Cloudmanager controllers (AWSKubernetesEngine, AzureKubernetesEngine, CoreWeaveKubernetesEngine) include `name`, `namespace`, `resourceKind` | RHAISTRAT-8010-E002 | cloudmanager binary; name/namespace already provided by the shared reconciler — remaining work is embedded-identity conversion plus `resourceKind` |\n126\t| HLR-6 | P0 | Module controller error logs (8 module handlers) include `name`, `namespace`, `resourceKind` | RHAISTRAT-8010-E001 | module controller tier added by SME input |\n127\t| HLR-7 | P1 | DSC and component controller reconciliation error logs include `resourceKind` | RHAISTRAT-8010-E001 (bundled) | Bundled with the P0 work: HLR-7 edits the same log.Error call sites in the same DSC/component controller files as the HLR-1 conversion pass; `resourceKind` is added in the same mechanical edit, so a split would double-touch the same files and duplicate the call-site audit. |\n128\t| HLR-8 | P1 | Document the convention as a contribution guideline and publish for external operator adoption | RHAISTRAT-8010-E004 | docs-authoring epic; priority from strategy frontmatter (Major → P1) per the docs-authoring priority exception |\n129\t\n130\tAll eight HLRs are covered; no orphaned requirements.\n131\t\n132\t## Health Warnings\n133\t\n134\t- **Planning note (epic size)**: the manager binary log standardization epic\n135\t  (RHAISTRAT-8010-E001) is ~1.25–1.5 sprints by the strategy's own effort estimate\n136\t  (0.5 sprint audit + 0.75–1 sprint conversion) — above the ~2-week epic-size guideline.\n137\t  Kept as one logical change: a split would duplicate the call-site audit and fragment a single\n138\t  mechanical conversion over one surface. **Re-split trigger**: re-split this epic if the audit\n139\t  finds materially more `log.Error` call sites than the strategy's 75%-of-calls estimate\n140\t  implies, or if PR-review throughput forces it — the natural split lines are the five\n141\t  controller tiers / the 15 action files.\n142\t- **External adoption is not controllable**: COO team adoption (Loki Operator, Tempo Operator)\n143\t  is voluntary per platform policy overlay 0008. The decomposition publishes the convention and\n144\t  files tracking issues; adoption timing and occurrence are external and must not be used as a\n145\t  gate or acceptance criterion on any epic.\n146\t- **Process gates pending**: all Prerequisites & Process Gates in the strategy (Architecture\n147\t  Review, Build Onboarding, Licence Validation, etc.) are \"Pending human review\". The component\n148\t  is established in the platform (architecture context exists; Konflux builds exist), so these\n149\t  read as strategy-signoff formalities rather than generators of onboarding epics — but they\n150\t  remain unresolved human gates on the strategy itself.\n151\t- **Log consumer regression risk**: message strings change (resource identity moves out of\n152\t  message text) and the DSCI key migration renames fields that existing Loki/CloudWatch queries\n153\t  may filter on. Mitigation is in scope as acceptance criteria on both implementation epics\n154\t  (before/after ingestion verification, SRE/Support alert-rule identification); the DSCI\n155\t  migration must land in a single release to avoid long-term inconsistency.\n156\t- **Open product decision**: whether the convention should grow fields beyond\n157\t  name/namespace/kind (reconciliation attempt count, error category) is unresolved (PM /\n158\t  Observability). The guideline documents the three required fields; an extension is follow-up\n159\t  scope and does not gate this decomposition.\n160\t\n161\t## Tiered Delivery\n162\t\n163\tNot applicable in the dependency-gated sense: no external dependency blocks RHOAI-side work\n164\t(the only external dependency, COO adoption, is out of RHOAI's control entirely and is not a\n165\tdeliverable of any epic). Sequencing flexibility exists by construction: the cloudmanager epic\n166\tis independent of the manager epic and has no downstream dependents besides the guideline, so\n167\tit can be scheduled into the same release or a subsequent one (the strategy's open release-timing\n168\tquestion) without restructuring the DAG.\n169\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-9c12a793744784a4","agent_id":"a3cf97bca7c0ca59c","session_id":"fa7e0b7e-f01f-4