{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-aad4d3c2ae62b84f","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t---\n2\tepic_id: RHAISTRAT-8010-E001\n3\ttitle: Manager binary structured reconciliation logging (name/namespace/resourceKind)\n4\tparent_strat: RHAISTRAT-8010\n5\tcomponent: OpenShift AI Productization\n6\tteam: ODH Operator team\n7\ttype: Implementation\n8\tpriority: P0\n9\tdependencies: null\n10\tai_signals:\n11\t  change_specificity: 1\n12\t  pattern_precedent: 1\n13\t  adapter_pattern: 0\n14\t  existing_foundation: 1\n15\t  open_questions: 1\n16\t  external_dependency: 1\n17\t  human_process_gates: -1\n18\t  repo_access: 1\n19\t  architecture_claims: 1\n20\timplementation_type: null\n21\tinvestigation_signals: null\n22\tai_implementability: High\n23\tai_implementability_score: 6\n24\tjira_key: null\n25\tbranch: null\n26\tgated_by: null\n27\tgate_failure_impact: null\n28\t---\n29\t# Manager binary structured reconciliation logging (name/namespace/resourceKind)\n30\t\n31\t## Description\n32\t\n33\tConverts all reconciliation error log calls in the rhods-operator manager binary to the\n34\tstructured logging convention: resource identity emitted as structured key-value pairs\n35\t(`name`, `namespace`, `resourceKind`, camelCase keys) instead of being embedded in message\n36\tstrings. This covers the three diagnosed problems in the manager binary: message-embedded\n37\tresource identity, the DSCInitialization controller's non-standard key names\n38\t(`Request.Name`/`DSCInitialization`), and the missing `resourceKind` field across all\n39\tcontroller tiers and action files.\n40\t\n41\t## Scope\n42\t\n43\t- Audit all `log.Error()` call sites in manager reconciliation error paths and track coverage\n44\t  in a checklist (grep/static analysis; the audit also confirms the strategy's 75%\n45\t  embedded-identity estimate).\n46\t- Convert every reconciliation error log call that embeds resource name or namespace in the\n47\t  message string to structured key-value pairs (`name`, `namespace`).\n48\t- Migrate the DSCInitialization (DSCI) controller from the non-standard key names\n49\t  (`Request.Name`, `DSCInitialization`) to the camelCase convention (`name`, `namespace`).\n50\t- Add `resourceKind` as a structured field to reconciliation error logs across all five\n51\t  manager controller tiers:\n52\t  - DataScienceCluster (DSC) controller\n53\t  - 8 component controllers (DataSciencePipelines, Kueue, ModelRegistry, Ray, SparkOperator,\n54\t    Trainer, TrainingOperator, TrustyAI)\n55\t  - Module controller with its 8 module handlers (Dashboard, AIGateway, KServe, OGX,\n56\t    Workbenches, MLflowOperator, FeastOperator, MCPLifecycleOperator)\n57\t  - DSCI controller\n58\t  - 5 service controllers (Gateway, Auth, Monitoring, CertConfigMapGenerator, Setup)\n59\t- Update the 15 action files across four tiers to emit `name`, `namespace`, and\n60\t  `resourceKind` on error logs:\n61\t  - 8 component action files (`components/<name>/<name>_controller_actions.go`)\n62\t  - 3 service action files (`services/{auth,gateway,monitoring}/*_controller_actions.go`)\n63\t  - 1 DSC action file (`datasciencecluster/datasciencecluster_controller_actions.go`)\n64\t  - 3 module action files (`modules/modules_controller_actions*.go`)\n65\t- Ensure cluster-scoped resources (e.g., DataScienceCluster, DSCInitialization) emit `name`\n66\t  and `resourceKind` with `namespace` omitted or set to empty string — never a spurious value.\n67\t- Add unit tests asserting field presence and correctness via test logger output for each\n68\t  controller tier and action file tier.\n69\t- Backwards-compatibility verification: before/after log output comparison for sample\n70\t  reconciliation errors, confirming structured fields appear and the production log consumer\n71\t  stack (Loki) ingests both formats without configuration changes; coordinate with\n72\t  SRE/Support to identify text-based alert rules that rely on the changed message strings and\n73\t  document the required updates.\n74\t- Draft the release-note entry documenting the DSCI key-name migration with before/after\n75\t  query examples (single-release transition).\n76\t\n77\t## Acceptance Criteria\n78\t\n79\t- Static analysis over the manager binary reports zero reconciliation-path `log.Error` calls\n80\t  embedding resource name or namespace in the message string.\n81\t- A codebase grep confirms no remaining use of the `Request.Name` / `DSCInitialization` field\n82\t  names in DSCI logging, and unit tests assert DSCI error logs contain `name`, `namespace`,\n83\t  and `resourceKind`.\n84\t- Unit tests assert each service controller's (Gateway, Auth, Monitoring,\n85\t  CertConfigMapGenerator, Setup) reconciliation error log includes `resourceKind` with the\n86\t  correct value.\n87\t- Unit tests assert error logs in all 15 action files across the four tiers include `name`,\n88\t  `namespace`, and `resourceKind` with correct values.\n89\t- Unit tests assert module controller error logs (including the 8 module handlers) include\n90\t  `name`, `namespace`, and `resourceKind`.\n91\t- Unit tests assert DSC and component controller reconciliation error logs include\n92\t  `resourceKind`.\n93\t- Unit tests confirm cluster-scoped resources emit `name` and `resourceKind` and no spurious\n94\t  `namespace` value.\n95\t- A before/after log output comparison for a sample reconciliation error is documented,\n96\t  showing structured fields present and both old and new formats ingested by the production\n97\t  log consumer without configuration changes.\n98\t- Text-based alert rules affected by the changed message strings are identified in\n99\t  coordination with SRE/Support, and the required rule updates are documented.\n100\t- A release note documenting the DSCI key-name migration with before/after query examples is\n101\t  drafted.\n102\t\n103\t## HLR Traceability\n104\t\n105\t- **HLR-1 (P0)**: conversion of message-embedded resource identity to structured KV pairs\n106\t  (`name`, `namespace`) — manager binary portion.\n107\t- **HLR-2 (P0)**: DSCI controller migration to camelCase key names.\n108\t- **HLR-3 (P0)**: `resourceKind` in service controller error logs.\n109\t- **HLR-4 (P0)**: `name`/`namespace`/`resourceKind` in all 15 action files across 4 tiers.\n110\t- **HLR-6 (P0)**: `name`/`namespace`/`resourceKind` in module controller error logs\n111\t  (8 module handlers).\n112\t- **HLR-7 (P1)**: `resourceKind` in DSC and component controller error logs — bundled with the\n113\t  P0 conversion because it edits the same `log.Error` call sites in the same files; a split\n114\t  would double-touch the same files and duplicate the call-site audit.\n115\t\n116\t## Risks / Notes\n117\t\n118\t- **Inconsistent adoption across controllers**: with five controller tiers and 15 action\n119\t  files, some error paths may be missed — mitigated by the grep/static-analysis audit\n120\t  checklist and the CI lint rule that enforces the convention going forward.\n121\t- **Log consumer regression**: existing log-based alerts may match on message strings that\n122\t  change; mitigated by the before/after ingestion verification and SRE/Support alert-rule\n123\t  identification called out in the acceptance criteria.\n124\t- **DSCI query breakage**: operators with existing Loki/CloudWatch queries filtering on\n125\t  `Request.Name`/`DSCInitialization` are affected; mitigated by the release note and the\n126\t  single-release transition.\n127\t- **Epic size**: per the strategy's effort estimate this epic is ~1.25–1.5 sprints — above the\n128\t  ~2-week guideline. Kept as one logical change: a split would duplicate the call-site audit\n129\t  and fragment a single mechanical conversion. Re-split trigger: re-split this epic if the\n130\t  audit finds materially more `log.Error` call sites than the strategy's 75%-of-calls estimate\n131\t  implies, or if PR-review throughput forces it.\n132\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-a7a6b45285b9a171","agent_id":"a65b604becdab9c7c","session_id":"fa7e0b7e-f01f-43d2-b53c-0d6134573784","uuid":"cfc14404-0bd4-4b38-b840-d92da3965fec","timestamp":"2026-10-25T20:53:09.280Z","tool_result_meta":[{"id":"chatcmpl-tool-aad4d3c2ae62b84f","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}],"subagent_type":"claude","task_description":"Re-review RHAISTRAT-8010"}
